 ],
 "literal": "Forest peacock on the rock laid egg / in the sun playing black monkey's young one rolls / hill country lord's friendship always / would be good indeed, may you live, friend, kohl-painted eyes / with tears together to fade / without thinking of him enduring to those who are able!",
 "translation": "The friendship of the lord of the hills, where the young of the sun-basking black monkey rolls the egg laid by a forest peacock on a flat rock, would indeed be sweet, my friend, but only for those who have the strength to endure without thinking of him, even as their beautiful kohl-lined eyes lose their luster and fill with tears.",
 "modern": "தோழியே! காட்டில் வாழும் மயில் பாறையின் மேல் இட்ட முட்டையை, வெயிலில் விளையாடும் கருங்குரங்கின் குட்டி உருட்டி விளையாடும் மலைநாட்டினுடைய தலைவனின் நட்பு எப்போதும் நல்லதுதான். ஆனால், அவனுடைய நினைவே இல்லாமல், அழகிய மை தீட்டிய கண்கள் கண்ணீரோடு ஒளியிழந்து வாடாதவாறு, அவனை நினைக்காமல் பொறுத்துக் கொள்ளும் வலிமை உடையவர்களுக்கு மட்டுமே அது நல்லதாக இருக்கும்.",
 "explanation": "In this Kuriñci poem, the heroine speaks to her confidante who is trying to console her during the hero's absence. She remarks with gentle irony that the hero's love is indeed wonderful, but only for women strong enough to live without constantly thinking of him and crying. The imagery of the young monkey carelessly rolling the peacock's egg on a rock serves as an implied metaphor (uḷḷuṟai). It suggests that the hero is playing carelessly with the heroine's delicate life and feelings, unaware of the danger of destroying them. This vivid scene of monkeys and peacocks perfectly evokes the mountainous Kuriñci landscape of love and waiting.",
